event('212-5.1-ii','independent re-proof','Integrality and the inclusion A in B give surjectivity and a closed, hence quotient, map on spectra by lying-over. Every relation orbit lies in a fibre. Conversely let primes p and q lie over the same prime of A, with q outside the finite orbit of p. Incomparability for integral extensions shows q is contained in none of the primes in that orbit; prime avoidance gives b in q outside all those primes. Its invariant norm along p1 of p2 prime(b), constructed as in 5.1(i), vanishes at q because the identity section is a factor, but is nonzero at p because b is a unit at every point of that finite fibre. This contradicts equality of the contractions to A. Thus the fibres are precisely the orbits. Lying-over, incomparability and prime avoidance are the standard algebraic inputs.','212, pp. 110–111, Theorem 5.1(ii)')
event('212-5.1-iii','targeted proof check','The equalizer A = Eq(B to C) localizes over A, so O_Y is the sheaf equalizer of f_*O_X to the two copies of g_*O_R1. An invariant map h:X to Z is constant on the fibres by (ii), hence descends to a continuous map because f has quotient topology. On the inverse images of affine opens of Z, the equalizer descends h on functions and these maps glue. Locality is detected after pulling back to any x over y: an element that were a unit at y would remain a unit at x, so a germ in the maximal ideal at h(x) cannot become a unit at y. The resulting morphism is unique. This verifies the coequalizer in all preschemes, not only in affine schemes.','212, p. 111, Theorem 5.1(iii)')
event('212-5.1-iv','carried as background without fault found','The effective finite locally free equivalence-relation assertion is retained, but a complete independent proof of this part has not yet been supplied. Its substantial algebraic step is to prove that B is a finite faithfully projective A-module and that B tensor_A B to C is an isomorphism when R1 to X times X is a monomorphism. The norm proof of integrality and the orbit proof alone do not establish those statements. The later notes must identify an exact source or give that remaining argument rather than label it proved here.','212, p. 111, Theorem 5.1(iv)')
event('212-5.2','independent re-proof','Check surjectivity on geometric points. Suppose two Omega-valued points of X over the same point of Y, with Omega algebraically closed, are in different relation orbits. The outgoing-arrow fibre is finite, so its orbit is finite. In B tensor_A Omega, the distinct Omega-points have distinct maximal ideals; Chinese remainders provide u vanishing at the second point and nonzero at every point of the first orbit. Write u as a finite sum of b_i with scalar coefficients c_i. Form the norm of p2 prime(sum b_i T_i) universally over B[T_i]. Its coefficients lie in A by the composition argument, because polynomial extension is flat and the groupoid acts on the outgoing arrows preserving targets. Specializing T_i to c_i therefore gives the same norm value at the two Omega-points. At the second it is zero by the identity section; at the first it is nonzero, since the corresponding element of its finite Omega-algebra is a unit. Contradiction. Thus every geometric pair has an arrow, proving R1 to X times_Y X surjective. This argument does not assume invariants commute with arbitrary, possibly nonflat, base change.','212, p. 111, Corollary 5.2')
event('212-5-global-equalizer','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement id: 212-5-global-equalizer-affine. The displayed sheaf exactness alone is insufficient. Take X=P^1_k with the identity relation R1=X, Y=Spec(k), and f the structure map. Then f_*O_X=k, both parallel maps are identities, and all the printed equalizer conditions hold. But f is not integral or the coequalizer of the identity pair (whose quotient is X). Adding that f is affine is sufficient: over every affine open of Y, X and R1 are affine and 5.1 applies, so the conclusions glue. The original has no word affine in this hypothesis; both editions preserve that omission because it is a substantive missing assumption, not an unambiguous typographical substitution.','212, p. 111, paragraph between Corollary 5.2 and Theorem 5.3')
event('212-5.3-definition-caveat','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement id: 212-5.3-affine. If admissible is interpreted literally as existence of the displayed sheaf equalizer alone, the stated criterion also fails. Let X be the affine line with doubled origin and let the constant group of order two exchange the two charts while acting identically on their common punctured line. Its action groupoid has finite locally free source projection. The natural f:X to A^1 has f_*O_X=O_A1 and both action maps induce the same functions, so the displayed equalizer holds. The orbit consisting of the two origins lies in no affine open: any open containing both has the two dense branches sharing their punctured overlap and is nonseparated, whereas an affine open is separated. Thus the intended admissibility needs the affine quotient condition, or equivalent full conclusions, rather than only the displayed exactness. This is a caveat about the incomplete preceding definition, not a change to the printed theorem.','212, p. 111, admissibility definition and Theorem 5.3')
event('212-5.3','independent re-proof','Stable-affine-neighbourhood step for the intended affine quotient criterion: let O be a finite orbit in an affine open U. The saturation of X minus U is closed because both projections are finite; its complement V is a saturated open inside U containing O. Prime avoidance gives h on U with O contained in D_U(h) contained in V. On V set a=N_p1(p2^*h). Composition makes a invariant, and it is nonzero on O. The identity section splits the finite locally free algebra and makes h a factor of its norm, so D_V(a) is contained in D_U(h). It is the principal open cut out by a restricted to the affine D_U(h), hence affine, saturated and containing O. Apply 5.1 on these saturated affine opens. Their quotients agree on overlaps by the sheaf equalizer and orbit description; saturated open images are open since the local integral quotient is closed. They therefore glue. Conversely an affine quotient has an affine inverse image of each affine neighbourhood of an orbit image. This proves the criterion with the necessary intended affine-quotient interpretation; part 5.1(iv) remains the separately identified background step when effectiveness is wanted.','212, p. 111, Theorem 5.3 and last paragraph')
event('212-5.3-quasi-projective-example','carried as background without fault found','The parenthetical example uses the standard ample-line-bundle fact that finitely many points of a quasi-projective scheme over an affine base admit a common affine neighbourhood. It is not merely a consequence of separatedness; higher powers of the ample bundle may be needed. No independent general proof of that auxiliary fact is claimed here.','212, p. 111, Theorem 5.3 parenthesis')
record_page('212',111,r'''Compared sources/fr_printed/fr/212.tex entry lines 556–603 and sources/fr/fr/212.tex entry lines 559–606 with scan leaf 14 at 400 dpi, full page and all three enlarged bands, plus a tight crop confirming the period in the number 5.2. Read all clauses of 5.1, Corollary 5.2, the displayed sheaf equalizer and Theorem 5.3.

Both layers restore the exact underline spans (relation alone and stable alone), ordinary quotes around the two phrases including admissible, script symbols for the whole groupoid, spacing, the ordinary sheaf arrow and upright mathematics. The sheaf O symbols and the ordinary R subscript in O_R are retained as printed. There is no mathematical difference between the two layers on this page.

Verified the orbit and categorical-quotient assertions and proved the geometric surjectivity in 5.2 using a universal norm, without assuming arbitrary base change for invariants. The effective finite flat relation step in 5.1(iv) is explicitly carried as background. A projective-line counterexample refutes the claimed globalization from sheaf exactness alone; a doubled-origin example exposes the same issue in a literal reading of admissible. The stable affine neighbourhood construction and gluing were checked for the precise affine-quotient refinement of 5.3. These qualifications are held for the notes and do not alter the historical text.''')
